  abstract: |
    Does telling a language model to consider the people its answer affects make its answers consider them more fully? Six models were given three ethical reasoning loops, a purpose check, a stakeholder-care check and a universalisability check, as a prefix in the prompt, against a template without ethical content, on ten prompts at four reasoning depths. The GPT-5.4 run failed in scoring. Under paired tests, stakeholder care rose significantly in all five other runs: Claude Opus 4.6 (+3.17, $d = 0.94$), DeepSeek V3.2 (+6.03, $d = 0.69$), Gemini 3 Flash (+13.50, $d = 1.14$) and Groq Qwen3 (+8.90, $d = 1.07$), each $p < 0.0001$, and Grok 4.1 Fast (+5.04, $d = 0.54$, $p = 0.0105$). The composite rose significantly on Gemini (+5.33, $p = 0.0018$) and Groq (+4.93, $p = 0.0014$), was positive but not significant on DeepSeek, flat on Claude and neutral on Grok, where intellectual honesty fell. The loops ask the model to list everyone affected, which the stakeholder-care rubric scores, so the rise cannot yet be told apart from following that instruction. Each run had one model scorer, which paraphrased what it scored; none was fully blind. A later five-judge DeepSeek run found care and its composite rising (+3.35, $p = 0.014$). No run tested a pre-training architecture or autonomous self-modification. The paper hypothesises that care leads a cascade into nuance, honesty and position quality, and that values acquired in formation resist later change. Modelling an evaluator does not entail evasion. A deliberate exploit requires a reachable mismatch or bypass, feasible search and incentives to use it. Value persistence remains untested, and human control should be retained. Five draft protocols address these hypotheses. It informs the design of the Eden Protocol and tests none of the ARC Theory’s five laws, each a law under test.

